# CAN CTF Writeup: Reverse Engineering a Rolling-Code Key Fob

## Overview

This challenge presented a simulated vehicle instrument cluster through a web application. The dashboard displayed live vehicle state including:

* Door lock state
* Immobiliser state
* Horn state
* Vehicle speed
* Turn signals
* CAN frames per second
* Number of observed CAN IDs
* A flag field released after satisfying the challenge condition

The supplied virtual key fob contained controls for:

* LOCK
* HORN
* IMMOB_ARM
* IMMOB_DISARM

Notably, the interface explicitly stated:

> Key fob — the buttons you have (no unlock)

The goal was therefore to determine how key-fob commands were represented on the CAN bus and ultimately construct a valid UNLOCK command.


## Initial Web Reconnaissance

The application exposed two obvious HTTP endpoints.

The dashboard received vehicle state from:

GET /events

using Server-Sent Events.

The virtual key-fob buttons submitted:

POST /press
Content-Type: application/json

with bodies such as:

{"button":"LOCK"}

Attempting to submit an obvious missing command:

{"button":"UNLOCK"}

returned:

{"msg":"no such control","ok":false}

This established that UNLOCK was not simply a hidden web control.

The dashboard HTML also contained several useful clues:

Instrument Cluster — live CAN
IDs seen: 12
Flag released on the bus


This strongly suggested that the intended solution involved interacting directly with CAN rather than bypassing the web interface.


## Discovering socketcand

A TCP port scan revealed:

22/tcp     SSH
8080/tcp   Werkzeug web application
29536/tcp  unknown

Connections to TCP port 29536 returned:

< hi >

This was identified as the greeting used by **socketcand**, which exposes Linux SocketCAN interfaces over TCP.

Opening the virtual CAN interface and switching to raw mode:

< open vcan0 >
< rawmode >

returned:

< ok >
< ok >

Live CAN traffic then became visible:

< frame 429 1789173655.554768 010000a500000000 >

This was the first major breakthrough.

## Identifying the Vehicle-State Frame

One frame appeared periodically with a notably simple payload:

429  01 00 00 a5 00 00 00 00

By pressing legitimate controls and observing this ID, the following relationship emerged:

01 00 00 a5 00 00 00 00
│  │  │
│  │  └─ Horn
│  └──── Immobiliser
└─────── Door lock

Examples included:

01 00 00 a5 00 00 00 00   LOCKED / DISARMED / HORN OFF
01 01 00 a5 00 00 00 00   LOCKED / ARMED / HORN OFF
01 00 01 a5 00 00 00 00   LOCKED / DISARMED / HORN ON

The obvious candidate for the unlocked state was therefore:

00 00 00 a5 00 00 00 00

Injecting that state frame caused the instrument cluster to briefly display:

UNLOCKED

Repeated transmission could even keep the dashboard showing an unlocked condition.
However, the challenge did **not** complete.
This was an important distinction: CAN ID 429 represented **vehicle status**, not the actual key-fob command that caused the vehicle state machine to transition.

## Finding the Key-Fob Command Frame

A small capture script was written to correlate each `/press` request with CAN activity immediately surrounding the button press.

One relatively quiet CAN ID appeared exactly once for every fob action:

12A

Representative captures were:

LOCK
12A  2c 3c 49 b0 ad dc 48 ef

HORN
12A  34 19 ea b0 f7 a0 48 ef

IMMOB_ARM
12A  3c 3e 89 b0 3f 1c 48 ef

IMMOB_DISARM
12A  40 f4 6e b0 57 95 48 ef

Repeated captures revealed a clear structure.

Byte 0 increased monotonically:

2c
2d
2e
2f
30
31
...

while byte 1 remained constant for a given command:

LOCK          = 3c
HORN          = 19
IMMOB_ARM     = 3e
IMMOB_DISARM  = f4

The apparent packet structure became:


Byte 0   Rolling counter
Byte 1   Command opcode
Byte 2   Changing value
Byte 3   Constant b0
Byte 4   Changing value
Byte 5   Changing value
Byte 6   Constant 48
Byte 7   Constant ef


## Testing Replay Protection

A valid HORN frame was captured and then retransmitted exactly.

The replay did not activate the horn.

Changing only the visible counter to the next value also failed.

This demonstrated that the receiver enforced freshness and that the additional changing fields participated in packet validation.

An 8-bit counter rollover experiment was also performed. Even after advancing the visible counter through a complete wrap, an old captured frame remained invalid.

This suggested that the visible counter represented only part of the receiver's freshness state.

## Concurrency Experiment

The next breakthrough came from issuing many legitimate /press requests at nearly the same instant.

A burst of concurrent requests produced frames similar to:

56 3e 65 b0 01 e8 48 ef
57 3c 65 b0 00 e8 48 ef
58 f4 65 b0 b9 e8 48 ef
59 19 65 b0 df e8 48 ef
5a f4 65 b0 bb e8 48 ef
...

Two observations were particularly important.

During the entire burst:

Byte 2 = 65
Byte 5 = e8

remained constant.

Under normal conditions, both values changed over time.

This strongly suggested that the two fields represented short-lived freshness material such as a timestamp-derived value or nonce.

More importantly, byte 4 was no longer mysterious.

Across every captured packet:

Byte4 =
    Byte0
  + Byte1
  + Byte2
  + Byte5
  + 0x20

with the result truncated to eight bits.

In other words:

checksum = (counter + command + freshness1 + freshness2 + 0x20) & 0xff

For example:

56 3e 65 b0 01 e8 48 ef

gives:

0x56 + 0x3e + 0x65 + 0xe8 + 0x20
= 0x201

0x201 & 0xff
= 0x01

which exactly matches the packet's checksum byte.

The concurrency experiment had effectively exposed the validation algorithm.

## Forging a Valid Known Command

Before attempting the missing command, the recovered packet format needed to be verified.

A fresh legitimate frame was captured to obtain:

current rolling counter
freshness byte A
freshness byte B

A new frame was then constructed for the **next** counter value using the already known IMMOB_ARM opcode.

The checksum was recalculated as:

checksum = (
    counter
    + command
    + freshness1
    + freshness2
    + 0x20
) & 0xff

The resulting forged packet had the form:

[counter]
[IMMOB_ARM]
[freshness1]
[b0]
[checksum]
[freshness2]
[48]
[ef]

After injection, the vehicle changed from:


IMMOBILISER: DISARMED

to:

IMMOBILISER: ARMED

without using the legitimate ARM control.

This confirmed that the protocol had been successfully reconstructed and that arbitrary valid fob commands could be generated as long as fresh state was used.

## Locating the Missing UNLOCK Command

At this stage, only a single unknown remained:

UNLOCK opcode = ?

The known command-byte values were:

HORN          = 0x19
LOCK          = 0x3c
IMMOB_ARM     = 0x3e
IMMOB_DISARM  = 0xf4

Because the command field was a single byte, the remaining search space was small.

For each candidate value, the test harness:

1. Generated a legitimate command to obtain current freshness material.
2. Captured the current rolling counter.
3. Advanced to the expected next counter value.
4. Inserted a candidate command byte.
5. Recalculated the checksum.
6. Injected the forged frame immediately.
7. Watched CAN ID 429 for a transition to the unlocked state.
8. Monitored the application's event stream for challenge completion.

The relevant logic was essentially:

for opcode in range(256):

    fresh = capture_legitimate_frame()

    counter = (fresh.counter + 1) & 0xff

    checksum = (
        counter
        + opcode
        + fresh.byte2
        + fresh.byte5
        + 0x20
    ) & 0xff

    forged = [
        counter,
        opcode,
        fresh.byte2,
        0xb0,
        checksum,
        fresh.byte5,
        0x48,
        0xef
    ]

    inject(forged)

    if vehicle_unlocked():
        print("UNLOCK command identified")
        break

One candidate eventually caused the genuine vehicle state machine to transition to:

UNLOCKED

and satisfied the challenge.

The actual opcode and resulting flag are deliberately omitted.

## Recovered Protocol Structure

The reconstructed key-fob frame was:

Byte 0   Rolling counter
Byte 1   Command opcode
Byte 2   Freshness value A
Byte 3   0xb0
Byte 4   8-bit additive checksum
Byte 5   Freshness value B
Byte 6   0x48
Byte 7   0xef

Checksum:

B4 = (B0 + B1 + B2 + B5 + 0x20) mod 256

Known command values:

LOCK          0x3c
HORN          0x19
IMMOB_ARM     0x3e
IMMOB_DISARM  0xf4
UNLOCK        <REDACTED>

## Lessons Learned

Several elements of the challenge were useful hints rather than decoration.

The phrase:

> Key fob — the buttons you have (no unlock)

suggested that UNLOCK existed at the protocol level even though the interface did not expose the corresponding control.

Likewise:

> Flag released on the bus

was a strong clue that manipulating only the displayed vehicle state would not satisfy the challenge.

The most important distinction was:

429 = vehicle state/status
12A = key-fob command

Spoofing the former changed what the instrument cluster displayed. Forging the latter caused the actual simulated vehicle logic to perform the operation.

The concurrency experiment proved particularly effective. By generating multiple legitimate commands during the same freshness interval, normally changing fields became constant, allowing the checksum relationship to become obvious.

What initially resembled a more sophisticated rolling-code authentication system ultimately reduced to:

freshness
+ rolling counter
+ command opcode
+ 8-bit additive checksum

Once the structure was understood, constructing fresh valid packets became straightforward.

